{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b906cfafe007054f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9007\n3\tscore: 10\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Decomposition summary DAG diagram is inconsistent with epic frontmatter:\n10\t    five frontmatter dependencies have no diagram edge (E001 -> BRANCH-A-E006, BRANCH-A-E007,\n11\t    BRANCH-A-E008, BRANCH-B-E003, BRANCH-B-E004). The frontmatter is correct (gated_by\n12\t    must be a member of dependencies; Rule 3 requires a blocking edge to all affected\n13\t    Implementations), but the mermaid diagram draws E001 gate edges only to first-level\n14\t    branch epics while also drawing other transitively-redundant edges (E001 -> A-E004,\n15\t    E001 -> A-E005). Add the five missing E001 edges to the diagram (or apply one\n16\t    consistent convention) so diagram and frontmatter match exactly.'\n17\t- severity: major\n18\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n19\t  description: BRANCH-A-E005 frontmatter sets ai_signals.open_questions=0 while its\n20\t    own ai-signals rationale states there is an unresolved question that could change\n21\t    the approach (confirming the Platform Operator ownership and extension surface\n22\t    before starting) — verbatim the rubric -1 condition (open questions that would\n23\t    change implementation approach). Set open_questions=-1 on BRANCH-A-E005; the pipeline\n24\t    recomputes the score.\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: Completeness\n27\t  description: Upstream Milvus release selection is referenced by BRANCH-A-E002 (tracking\n28\t    the upstream release chosen for productization) and BRANCH-A-E003 (scanning the\n29\t    selected upstream release) but no epic explicitly owns making that selection;\n30\t    make it an acceptance criterion on BRANCH-A-E002.\n31\terror: null\n32\t---\n33\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9007 (Bring-your-own vector database for RAG workloads)\n34\t\n35\t## Review Summary\n36\t\n37\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n38\tRecommendation: revise\n39\tContract validator: OK (no violations).\n40\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition); multi-epic output across two\n41\tconditional branches is expected and correct.\n42\t\n43\tTwo criteria scored 0 (DAG Coherence, AI Implementability Scoring); the auto-fail rule\n44\tapplies regardless of the total score.\n45\t\n46\t## Criterion Details\n47\t\n48\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n49\t\n50\tAll five HLRs are covered, and the traceability matrix is present. HLR-1 (P0) maps to\n51\tE001; HLR-2 through HLR-5 are conditional on the posture decision and are covered in\n52\tboth branches: HLR-2 by BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E002, HLR-3 by BRANCH-A-E006 /\n53\tBRANCH-B-E003, HLR-4 by BRANCH-A-E007 / BRANCH-B-E002, HLR-5 by BRANCH-A-E008 /\n54\tBRANCH-B-E004. The strategy's own wording (\"Depending on that decision\") makes the\n55\tconditional mapping correct rather than a gap.\n56\t\n57\tPriority inheritance is sound: E001 (P0) gates everything; BRANCH-A-E003 (P0) blocks\n58\tBRANCH-A-E005 (P0); no lower-priority epic blocks P0 work. Both docs-authoring epics\n59\ttake P1 from the strategy's `priority: Major` per the documented exception, and their\n60\tRule 11 edges do not trigger priority inheritance.\n61\t\n62\tThe consolidation of HLR-2 and HLR-4 into BRANCH-B-E002 is accepted: on the external\n63\tpath both HLRs describe the same connection-management surface for the same component\n64\tand team (the registration capability is delivered through that UI), so HLR-4 is not a\n65\tdistinct deferrable feature — this is redundant-requirement wording, not priority\n66\tcollapse. In Branch A the two HLRs land on different components/teams and are\n67\tcorrectly split.\n68\t\n69\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n70\t\n71\tCritical issue — the decomposition summary DAG diagram is inconsistent with the epic\n72\tfrontmatter. Five frontmatter dependencies have no corresponding edge in the mermaid\n73\tdiagram:\n74\t\n75\t- E001 → BRANCH-A-E006 (frontmatter lists E001; diagram shows only A-E005 → A-E006)\n76\t- E001 → BRANCH-A-E007 (frontmatter lists E001; diagram shows only A-E005 → A-E007)\n77\t- E001 → BRANCH-A-E008 (frontmatter lists E001; diagram shows only the six A-impl edges)\n78\t- E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 (frontmatter lists E001; diagram shows only B-E002 → B-E003)\n79\t- E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 (frontmatter lists E001; diagram shows only B-E002/B-E003 → B-E004)\n80\t\n81\tThe frontmatter is correct per the rules — every branch epic must include the gating\n82\tInvestigation in `dependencies` (`gated_by` is always a member of `dependencies`), and\n83\tRule 3 requires a blocking edge from the Investigation to all affected Implementations.\n84\tThe diagram is the inconsistent artifact: it draws the E001 gate edges only to the\n85\tfirst-level branch epics (A-E002..A-E005, B-E002) and omits the five second-level gate\n86\tedges, while simultaneously drawing other transitively-redundant edges (E001 → A-E004\n87\tand E001 → A-E005 are both implied via A-E002 / A-E003). No consistent diagram\n88\tconvention (full direct-dependency drawing or transitive reduction) reproduces what is\n89\tdrawn. The DAG Justification table mirrors the diagram, so it also does not account for\n90\tthese five frontmatter edges. Fix: draw all five missing E001 edges in the diagram (or\n91\tstate and apply one consistent convention), keeping the diagram and frontmatter in\n92\texact correspondence.\n93\t\n94\tEverything else in the DAG is correct and was verified edge by edge:\n95\t\n96\t- No circular dependencies; every diagram edge has a matching frontmatter entry; all\n97\t  frontmatter dependencies reference existing epics.\n98\t- Implementation-type parallelism verified for every pair: no license-validation →\n99\t  repo-onboarding edge (Rule 7), no license-validation → konflux-onboarding edge\n100\t  (Rule 8), and no konflux-onboarding → general-implementation edge (Rule 10) —\n101\t  A-E004 and A-E005 are correctly parallel, with the coupling enforced by the Rule 25\n102\t  \"build pipeline green\" AC on A-E005 rather than a DAG edge.\n103\t- The productized-image digest consumed by A-E005 is correctly treated as a\n104\t  configuration reference (Rule 13): it appears as a \"pinned digest available at\n105\t  deployment time\" AC, not as an A-E004 → A-E005 dependency edge.\n106\t- All branch files set both `branch` and `gated_by`; no main-plan epic lists a branch\n107\t  epic in its `dependencies` (E001 has none).\n108\t- Critical path length 5 (E001 → A-E003 → A-E005 → A-E006/A-E007 → A-E008) fits the\n109\t  M-with-new-component heuristic (4-5).\n110\t\n111\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n112\t\n113\tDifferent component/team tuples produce separate epics throughout. The four\n114\tPlatform-Operator/ODH-Platform epics (A-E002..A-E005) share a tuple but are the\n115\tRule 20-mandated onboarding chain (repo-onboarding, license-validation,\n116\tkonflux-onboarding, general implementation), so separate epics are required, not a\n117\tboundary violation. No epic bundles multiple teams. No scope duplication: A-E006 vs\n118\tB-E003 (provider integration) and A-E007 vs B-E002 (Dashboard surface) are mutually\n119\texclusive branch alternatives, not independently rebuilt surfaces. Code\n120\timplementation and content authoring are never mixed — documentation is isolated in\n121\tthe two docs-authoring epics. No epic exceeds plausible sprint scale. The Milvus\n122\tmapping under the canonical `Platform Operator` component is documented as a health\n123\twarning and passes the contract validator.\n124\t\n125\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n126\t\n127\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: the posture outcome changes which downstream epics\n128\texist, and the conditional-branch modeling matches the strategy's explicit fork. Every\n129\tImplementation epic produces a concrete artifact. Every branch epic has `gated_by:\n130\tRHAISTRAT-9007-E001`, that target appears in its direct `dependencies`, and\n131\t`gate_failure_impact` is fully populated (`action: remove` with a concrete\n132\t`fallback_approach`) — `remove` is the correct action for mutually exclusive branches.\n133\tNo epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`.\n134\t\n135\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (0/2)\n136\t\n137\tMajor issue — BRANCH-A-E005's `open_questions` value contradicts both the rubric and\n138\tits own rationale. The frontmatter assigns `open_questions: 0`, but the ai-signals\n139\trationale states the epic's scope \"requires confirming the Platform Operator's\n140\townership and extension surface with the owning team before starting — an unresolved\n141\tquestion that could change the approach, so this epic does not meet the 'no open\n142\tquestions change the approach' bar.\" That is verbatim the rubric's −1 condition\n143\t(\"Open questions that would change implementation approach\"); the value must be −1,\n144\tnot 0. The rest of the decomposition confirms the scale's use (e.g. A-E006's\n145\t\"approach is settled\" → 1), so this is an inconsistency, not a different convention.\n146\tCorrection: set `ai_signals.open_questions=-1` on BRANCH-A-E005 (the pipeline will\n147\trecompute the score).\n148\t\n149\tAll other signals check out. E001 correctly uses the 5-signal Investigation rubric:\n150\t`source_accessibility: 0` is right (the answer is an undecided product posture, not in\n151\tany readable artifact), `human_judgment_required: -2` is right (the gating question is\n152\ta product/governance call), `question_specificity: 1` and `local_runnability: 1` are\n153\tjustified. Implementation epics all use the 9-signal rubric with present, substantive\n154\trationale files; the borderline-looking 0 values elsewhere (A-E002/A-E003\n155\t`open_questions` pending upstream release selection) are defensible under the\n156\t+1/0/−1 conditions, and `architecture_claims` (A-E005 −1 unsubstantiated operator\n157\tclaim vs A-E006/B-E003 +1 documented by architecture context) is consistently applied.\n158\t\n159\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n160\t\n161\tEvery epic has testable, epic-scoped acceptance criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are\n162\tpresent wherever applicable: both docs-authoring epics carry the Rule 24\n163\t\"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC; BRANCH-A-E005 (the epic with\n164\tkonflux-onboarding in its dependency chain) carries the Rule 25 \"build pipeline\n165\tgreen\" AC; Rule 23 rollback/feature-flag is N/A (new capability, no replacement) and\n166\tA-E007 still includes a feature-flag AC. No schedule-constraint ACs (lab turnaround,\n167\treview-board cadence) were found. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n168\t\n169\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n170\t\n171\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope in both bounded outcomes,\n172\tand the two branches exactly cover the strategy's two postures — nothing from the\n173\tout-of-scope list (shipping both postures, embedding models) leaks in, and the\n174\tsummary explicitly warns against cross-branch scope creep. The strategy's single open\n175\tquestion is modeled as the gating Investigation. Strategy-level context is carried\n176\tforward: the missing strategy sections, the Platform Operator inventory gap, and the\n177\tMilvus component mapping are all recorded as health warnings rather than silently\n178\tdropped. One minor nit: the upstream Milvus release selection is referenced by\n179\tBRANCH-A-E002 (\"the upstream release chosen for productization\") and BRANCH-A-E003\n180\t(\"the selected upstream Milvus release\") but no epic explicitly owns making that\n181\tselection — it is implicit in A-E002's scope and should be stated there as an AC.\n182\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90d5cd45208e6cee","agent_id":"a61f4abe16c2b3c47","session_id":"456aa445-a015-4ed6-b66f